  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/1807.01541v6</id>
    <title>Tensor Decomposition for EEG Signal Retrieval</title>
    <updated>2019-06-29T13:01:09Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.01541v6" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1807.01541v6" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Prior studies have proposed methods to recover multi-channel electroencephalography (EEG) signal ensembles from their partially sampled entries. These methods depend on spatial scenarios, yet few approaches aiming to a temporal reconstruction with lower loss. The goal of this study is to retrieve the temporal EEG signals independently which was overlooked in data pre-processing. We considered EEG signals are impinging on tensor-based approach, named nonlinear Canonical Polyadic Decomposition (CPD). In this study, we collected EEG signals during a resting-state task. Then, we defined that the source signals are original EEG signals and the generated tensor is perturbed by Gaussian noise with a signal-to-noise ratio of 0 dB. The sources are separated using a basic non-negative CPD and the relative errors on the estimates of the factor matrices. Comparing the similarities between the source signals and their recovered versions, the results showed significantly high correlation over 95%. Our findings reveal the possibility of recoverable temporal signals in EEG applications.</summary>
    <category term="eess.SP" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2018-07-04T12:32:37Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="eess.SP"/>
    <arxiv:journal_ref>2019 IEEE International Conference on Systems, Man and Cybernetics (SMC)</arxiv:journal_ref>
    <author>
      <name>Zehong Cao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mukesh Prasad</name>
    </author>
